  browser, and a [38;5;153m<script>[39m-global in [38;5;153msrc/[39m is invisible to Node. The tracking module has to live in one of these worlds or
  bridge both, and callers bake that choice into how they import it. Changing it later is a mechanical edit to every
  file, plus [38;5;153mindex.html[39m.

  - [1mConvert the browser side to ES modules[0m — [38;5;153mindex.html[39m gets [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, [38;5;153mapp.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/tracking.js[39m use
    [38;5;153mimport[39m/[38;5;153mexport[39m. Buys you one modern module system, no build step, and clean testability. Costs: ESM is blocked by
    CORS over [38;5;153mfile://[39m, so you'd need to serve the page ([38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m or similar) instead of double-clicking
    [38;5;153mindex.html[39m. If you currently open the file directly, this breaks that habit.
  - [1mDual-target module (UMD-ish)[0m — [38;5;153msrc/tracking.js[39m attaches to [38;5;153mwindow.Tracking[39m when in a browser and also sets
    [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m for Node. Buys you zero disruption: [38;5;153mfile://[39m keeps working, CommonJS keeps working. Costs: boilerplate
    in the module, a global on [38;5;153mwindow[39m, and a pattern that ages badly.
  - [1mAdd a bundler[0m — esbuild/Vite, one real toolchain. Buys you unrestricted imports and a path to TypeScript. Costs: the
    repo's first dependency and a build step for a four-file project. Hard to justify at this size.

  [1mMy recommendation: ES modules, if you're willing to serve the page.[0m It's the only option that doesn't leave a
  workaround in the code permanently. If [38;5;153mfile://[39m matters to you, say so and I'll take the dual-target route — that
  constraint outranks my preference.

